    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Equip Foods makes one protein powder and makes it very simply. Prime Protein is grass-fed beef protein isolate, about 21 g of protein per scoop for roughly 100 calories, with cocoa and stevia in the chocolate and nothing at all in the unflavored. For people who cannot have dairy, follow a paleo or carnivore-style diet, or just want a three-ingredient label, it is one of the better beef proteins on Amazon. It is also expensive per gram, lower in leucine than whey, and its chocolate flavor drew one of the highest lead results in Consumer Reports' 2025 protein testing, which Equip disputes.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Equip Prime Protein is a good product for a narrow set of buyers. It gives about 21 g of protein per scoop from grass-fed beef isolate for roughly 100 calories and about 2 g of carbohydrate, per the brand and retail listings at the time of writing. The unflavored version has one ingredient, beef. The chocolate has three: beef protein, cocoa and stevia. Labels rarely get shorter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those buyers are people who cannot or will not drink dairy and do not want plant protein: anyone with a milk allergy, people on paleo, carnivore or low-FODMAP diets, and people who find that both whey and pea protein upset their stomach. For them, a clean beef isolate solves a real problem, and Equip is one of the most established brands making one.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, there are three reasons to hesitate. It costs more per 25 g of protein than most whey. Its protein is rich in collagen-type amino acids, so it carries less leucine, the amino acid most tied to muscle building, than whey at the same dose. And in October 2025 Consumer Reports measured Equip Prime Protein chocolate at about 144% of its daily level of concern for lead per serving, among the highest in that report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Equip says CR tested older 2024 lots and that its current chocolate and unflavored lots test far lower. We lay out both sides below. Our verdict: if you need dairy-free animal protein, Equip is a reasonable buy, and the unflavored version is the one we would choose, both for its single ingredient and for the lowest lead figure Equip publishes. If you eat dairy without trouble, whey isolate is cheaper and better for muscle.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A clean dairy-free animal protein, at a price</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy Unflavored Prime Protein if you need beef protein. Buy whey isolate if you do not. Read the lead section before making the chocolate a daily habit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Equip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Equip Foods is a small, direct-to-consumer brand built around one flagship, Prime Protein. It sells through its own site, on Amazon and through retailers such as The Vitamin Shoppe, and it markets the powder squarely at keto, paleo and carnivore-style eaters, for whom a dairy-free, grain-free animal protein has an obvious audience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Around that flagship, Equip has added a handful of products: a grass-fed collagen, a colostrum supplement, a pre-workout, and in July 2025 the Prime Bar, a beef protein bar with about 20 g of protein made with beef protein, collagen, colostrum, dates, honey and tallow. Equip says the bar took 18 months of testing and reformulation, and it is made without seed oils or refined sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand's quality claims are its own. Equip says every batch of Prime Protein is tested by an independent lab for heavy metals, pesticides and other contaminants, and it publishes lot results on its site. The Amazon listing says its pasture-raised, grass-fed bovine ingredients are sourced primarily from the US and Sweden. What we could not confirm is a banned-substance certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            That combination is typical of smaller clean-label brands: more transparency than most big brands offer, but no third-party seal that a tested athlete can rely on. If you compete in a tested sport, that is the deciding factor, and our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder</a> guide notes which alternatives make stronger testing claims.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-its-made" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Beef Protein Isolate Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beef protein isolate is not powdered steak. Equip says it makes Prime Protein much the way bone broth is made: grass-fed beef, mostly lean muscle with some bone, is heated gently so the protein dissolves into a broth, then the fat is removed and the result is freeze-dried into a powder. The product is defatted, so it carries little fat or cholesterol, and Equip says the processing removes most of the beefy flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the process pulls protein from connective tissue and bone as well as muscle, the powder is naturally rich in collagen, gelatin and the amino acids glycine and proline. Equip markets that as a bonus for joints, skin and gut health, and it is one reason the product overlaps with <a href="/best-bone-broth-protein-powder/">bone broth protein powders</a> and <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen powders</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
