        <div id="lead-testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Consumer Reports Lead Dispute
          </h2>

          <p>
            In October 2025 Consumer Reports published lead results for 23 protein powders and ready-to-drink shakes. It measured each product against a level of concern of 0.5 micrograms of lead per day, a deliberately conservative threshold, and sorted products into those it was comfortable with daily, those it said were fine occasionally through the week, and those to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Equip Prime Protein chocolate came in at about 144% of that level of concern per serving and was placed in the occasional group, alongside products such as Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein and Vega Premium Sport. Coverage of the report also listed it with a weekly limit of a little under five servings. In plain terms, CR suggests a few chocolate shakes a week rather than one or two a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Equip disputes the comparison. It says CR tested only its chocolate flavor while some other brands were tested in vanilla or unflavored versions, a fair point given that cocoa is a known source of lead, and that the tested lots were from 2024. Equip says a September 2025 chocolate lot tested at about 11 ppb of lead against the 27 ppb CR reported, and that its current unflavored lot tested below about 2 ppb, well within California's Prop 65 limits. These are company figures from its own certificates of analysis.
          </p>

          <p>
            How to read that: both things can be true. Cocoa genuinely carries lead, which is why chocolate protein powders score worse across the market, and lot-to-lot variation is real. But CR's result is independent and Equip's newer numbers are not. Our practical advice is to choose unflavored or a non-cocoa flavor if you use Prime Protein daily, keep chocolate for occasional use, and check Equip's published lot results. Pregnant readers and parents should talk to a doctor first. Our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers heavy metals in more depth, and our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a> covers the plant powder that scored similarly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="beef-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Beef Protein vs Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you can drink whey comfortably, it is the better protein for building muscle. Whey isolate gives about 25 g of protein per scoop with roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine, digests quickly and costs less per 25 g than beef isolate. The small number of studies comparing beef and whey supplements in training programs have generally found similar gains when total protein is adequate, but whey reaches a leucine-rich dose with less powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beef isolate wins on tolerance. It contains no lactose and no milk proteins, so it suits people with a milk allergy, which a whey isolate cannot fix, and people whose stomachs react to dairy even in isolate form. It is also free of the legumes, gums and fibers that cause bloating with some plant proteins. For that group, a clean beef powder can be the difference between using a protein supplement and not using one.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are diet-fit reasons too. Paleo and carnivore-style eaters often avoid both dairy and legumes, which leaves beef, egg and collagen as the main powdered options. Of those, beef isolate gives the most protein per scoop with naturally occurring collagen alongside. Egg white protein is another dairy-free option with more leucine than beef, and it is often cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: whey if you tolerate it, beef if you need dairy-free animal protein, and a quality plant blend if you are vegan. If you choose beef, make up for its lower leucine with a slightly larger serving, about 25 to 30 g of protein, or by having it alongside a protein-rich meal. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison covers the other side of the decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Equip if you are a drug-tested athlete. Equip's independent batch testing is aimed at contaminants such as heavy metals and pesticides, and we could not confirm a banned-substance certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. A certified product is the safer route for anyone subject to testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you tolerate whey and your main goal is building muscle on a budget. You will pay more per 25 g for a protein with less leucine. A whey isolate, or a good whey concentrate if lactose is not an issue, gives you more muscle-building value per dollar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are vegetarian or vegan, obviously, and think twice if you are sensitive to red meat products for any reason, including the rare red meat allergy linked to tick bites, alpha-gal syndrome. Equip notes the product contains small amounts of Neu5Gc, a sugar molecule found in red meat and dairy, which matters only to people who have been told to avoid it.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip the chocolate as a daily shake if lead exposure worries you, or if you are pregnant or buying for a child, until you have looked at Equip's current lot results and spoken to your doctor. Anyone with kidney disease or gout should get a protein target from their doctor before adding any protein powder, beef included.
          </p>
        </div>

